Characteristics and parameters of Op-Amp(Virtual Experiment)

1. Aim

To study and measure the following fundamental characteristics and parameters of the IC-741 Operational Amplifier using the Virtual Labs simulation environment:

Input bias current at the inverting terminal(IB1)

Input bias current at the non-inverting terminal(IB2)

Input offset current(Iio)

Input offset voltage(Vio)

Slew Rate(SR)

An operational amplifier is a direct-coupled high-gain amplifier consisting of differential amplifiers followed by a level translator and a push-pull output stage.

While an ideal op-amp has infinite gain and bandwidth, practical ICs like the μA741 have specific limitations defined by their parameters.

Key Parameter Definitions:
--Input Offset Voltage (Vio):

The differential DC voltage required between the input terminals to force the output voltage to zero. For a 741C, the maximum value is typically 6mV.

--Input Bias Current (IB): The average of the currents flowing into the inverting (IB1) and non-inverting (IB2) terminals.

Formula:

IB=(IB1+IB2)/2

--Input Offset Current (Iio):

The algebraic difference between the currents entering the two input terminals.Formula: Iio = |IB1 - IB2|

--Slew Rate (SR):

The maximum rate of change of output voltage per unit of time, expressed in V/Us. 
It determines how fast the op-amp can respond to a rapid change at the input.

Formula: SR = dVo/dt |max
For IC-741, the typical Slew Rate is 0.5 V/µs.

